Fiji earthquake sample explorer
========================================================
author: Igor Grybek — AI-assisted draft
date: 15 October 2026

Explore a historical dataset without writing code.

The user problem
========================================================
- A table of 1,000 events is hard to explore quickly.
- Users want to restrict magnitude and depth, then see what remains.
- This teaching application makes the filters and calculations explicit.

Simple controls, reactive results
========================================================
- Set minimum magnitude and maximum depth.
- Select the histogram bin count.
- server.R filters the data and calculates the count, median magnitude and mean depth.
- Results, histogram and data preview update immediately.

A reproducible calculation
========================================================
```{r example, echo=TRUE}
d <- subset(datasets::quakes, mag >= 5 & depth <= 300)
data.frame(events=nrow(d),median_magnitude=median(d$mag),mean_depth_km=round(mean(d$depth),1))
```
The deployed application uses the same filtering rule with slider inputs.

Try it and interpret responsibly
========================================================
- Begin with magnitude 4.0 and depth 680 km to show all records.
- Increase the magnitude threshold and compare the count and histogram.
- The documentation is included on the application itself.
- Historical teaching sample; no live monitoring or forecast.
- Source: R datasets::quakes, Harvard PRIM-H / John Woodhouse.
